GX draw encoding without redundant state calls (render_seq). Each recorded GX draw used to set all
of its pass state (SetImmediates, the uniform and texture bind groups, SetIndexBuffer bound to the
draw's range, the blend constant) before its pipeline and draw. At the original frame rate the
early-Z split (0170/0179) runs per draw, unmerged, so every GX draw becomes a depth-only and a colour
command with identical state: about 40,000 commands per frame in LM's opening forest and the Parlor,
50-90 ms of render-worker time per game frame, and the game (lockstep) fell to 18-23 fps.
render_pass now encodes runs of GX draws with gx::render_seq: a state call is issued only when it
differs from what the previous GX draw set, and the whole index buffer stays bound with firstIndex
selecting the draw's range. The GPU receives the same state for every draw (frame dumps
byte-identical). Any other command type resets the tracked state. AURORA_GX_SEQ=0: the old path.

